WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:07.850
Now that we've seen the nitration mechanism,
let's talk a little bit more about this derivative

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:10.059
we've made.

00:00:10.059 --> 00:00:13.080
Nitrobenzene is less reactive than benzene.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:16.340
Remember that positive charge on the nitrogen
atom?

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:22.779
Well that is inductively electron withdrawing
and it pulls electron density out of the ring.

00:00:22.779 --> 00:00:28.539
Since benzene needs to use its electrons to
react in electrophilic aromatic substitution,

00:00:28.539 --> 00:00:32.430
they are now less available due to this inductive
effect.

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:37.940
We call the nitro group a deactivating group,
because it deactivates the benzene ring toward

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:42.070
further electrophilic aromatic substitution
reactions.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:47.700
We can also show how the nitro group pulls
electrons by drawing resonance structures.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:52.430
We can actually use the conjugation of the
aromatic ring with the double bonded oxygen

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:56.840
of the nitro group to show how electrons can
be pulled out of the ring.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:01.850
Now, we're just moving electrons around, so
the molecule needs to stay overall neutral,

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:06.710
and in this resonance form that we draw, we
end up with two positive charges and two formal

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:07.999
negative charges.

00:01:07.999 --> 00:01:14.740
Now, of course, this charge separated resonance
form is not the best resonance form for nitrobenzene,

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.570
but it does contribute.

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:21.929
Also, it gives important insight into nitrobenzene's
reactivity.

00:01:21.929 --> 00:01:26.479
Looking at an animation of how this resonance
occurs, notice that when a pair of electrons

00:01:26.479 --> 00:01:32.970
moves away from any one of the carbon atoms,
we gain formal positive charge on that carbon.

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:39.509
We can also represent the delocalization in
nitrobenzene using a resonance hybrid.

00:01:39.509 --> 00:01:45.679
Resonance hybrids aim to combine all of these
individual resonance forms into one structure.

00:01:45.679 --> 00:01:51.710
This is to better represent that the overall
structure is made up of each of these resonance

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:57.499
forms—that one resonance form doesn't exist
at one time in a molecule—any given molecule's

00:01:57.499 --> 00:02:02.530
actual structure is really an average of all
of its resonance structures.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:07.439
When we examine our resonance forms, we see
that our oxygen atom on the right hand side

00:02:07.439 --> 00:02:09.990
always maintains its negative charge.

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:16.550
So, we can draw a formal negative charge on
that oxygen atom in our resonance hybrid.

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:21.260
When we look at the nitrogen atom, we see
that it always stays with a formal positive

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:25.700
charge, so in our resonance hybrid, it will
maintain that positive charge.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:30.680
Now, the oxygen atom on the left hand side
is sometimes double bonded and sometimes single

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:31.680
bonded.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:37.810
So, here, we need to use a partial bond—a
dotted bond—to represent that double bond.

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:43.840
We'll also put a delta minus, since it's not
always formally negatively charged.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:48.260
The bond connecting the nitrogen to the ring
is sometimes single bonded, and sometimes

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:49.330
double bonded.

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:52.920
So, we'll use our dotted double bond convention
here as well.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:58.950
Now, within the ring, each carbon atom is
sometimes single bonded and sometimes double

00:02:58.950 --> 00:02:59.950
bonded.

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:05.200
So, we can draw dotted bonds within the ring,
all the way around the ring, to represent

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:06.200
this well.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:12.329
Finally, we can put delta positive charges
on three of our carbon atoms that get a formal

00:03:12.329 --> 00:03:16.200
positive charge in one of our resonance structures.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.560
And that generates our resonance hybrid.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:24.340
We can see the significance of this analysis
when we react nitrobenzene in an electrophilic

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:27.890
aromatic substitution reaction.

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:33.569
Electrophiles are positive, so these three
carbon atoms with positive character are going

00:03:33.569 --> 00:03:37.870
to have a repulsive interaction with an incoming
electrophile.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:42.439
Therefore, the electrophile is going to bond
at one of these carbon atoms that does not

00:03:42.439 --> 00:03:47.620
have positive character, and we can show our
electrophile bonding to just one of them in

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:49.530
our reaction scheme.

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:54.840
On a substituted benzene, we can define the
position that a new electrophile attaches

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:57.040
with respect to that group.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:04.110
The adjacent positions are the ortho positions,
next to those are two meta positions, and

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:07.409
across from the group, there is one para position.

00:04:07.409 --> 00:04:10.889
So, we can say the nitro group is a meta director.

